OK, `SIZEOF_ARRAY` is correct. Let me think about this from a completely different angle.

The crash is "Global-buffer-overflow READ 4 in md_start_new_block". The `switch(line->type)` reads 4 bytes from `line`. If `line` points to `md_dummy_blank_line` (a global), reading `line->type` is within the global. But if `line` points past the end of `md_dummy_blank_line`, that's a global-buffer-overflow.

Wait, could `line` be `&md_dummy_blank_line`? Let me look at the `md_process_doc` function again:

```c
const MD_LINE_ANALYSIS* pivot_line = &md_dummy_blank_line;
MD_LINE_ANALYSIS line_buf[2];
MD_LINE_ANALYSIS* line = &line_buf[0];
```

`line` is always `&line_buf[0]` or `&line_buf[1]`. It's never `&md_dummy_blank_line`.

But wait, what if `md_analyze_line` modifies `line` to point to `md_dummy_blank_line`? Let me check if `md_analyze_line` can modify the `line` pointer.

Looking at the signature: `md_analyze_line(MD_CTX* ctx, OFF beg, OFF* p_off, const MD_LINE_ANALYSIS* pivot_line, MD_LINE_ANALYSIS* line)`. The `line` parameter is a pointer to `MD_LINE_ANALYSIS`. Inside the function, `line` is used to write to the struct, but the pointer itself is not modified.

Hmm, let me reconsider. Maybe the issue is that `line` points to `md_dummy_blank_line` through some other mechanism. Let me look at the `md_process_line` function again:

```c
if(line->type == MD_LINE_TABLEUNDERLINE) {
    ...
    MD_ASSERT(pivot_line != &md_dummy_blank_line);
    ((MD_LINE_ANALYSIS*)pivot_line)->type = MD_LINE_TABLE;
    ...
}
```

If `pivot_line` points to `md_dummy_blank_line`, the `MD_ASSERT` would fail in debug builds. But in release builds, `((MD_LINE_ANALYSIS*)pivot_line)->type = MD_LINE_TABLE;` writes to the global. This is a write, not a read.

But wait, what if `pivot_line` points to `md_dummy_blank_line` and then `line` is set to `pivot_line`? Let me look at the `md_process_doc` loop:

```c
while(off < ctx->size) {
    if(line == pivot_line)
        line = (line == &line_buf[0] ? &line_buf[1] : &line_buf[0]);

    MD_CHECK(md_analyze_line(ctx, off, &off, pivot_line, line));
    MD_CHECK(md_process_line(ctx, &pivot_line, line));
}
```

If `pivot_line` is `&md_dummy_blank_line`, then `line != pivot_line` (since `line` is `&line_buf[0]` or `&line_buf[1]`). So `line` stays the same.

OK, I'm going in circles. Let me try a different approach. Let me look at the actual OSS-Fuzz issue to understand the exact crash. Let me search for it.

Actually, let me try to reproduce the crash with different inputs. The fix commit mentions two regression tests:
1. `    codeblock\n> [!note]\n> foo\n`
2. `> [!noteX]\n> foo\n`

The second one is interesting - `[!noteX]` has a tag that doesn't match any of the known tags. The fix adds a length check to prevent `md_ascii_case_eq` from reading past the end of the line. But `[!noteX]` is 8 characters, and the longest tag is "important" (9 characters). So `md_ascii_case_eq` would read 9 characters from `STR(line->beg+2)`, which is `"noteX]\n> foo\n"` - this reads past the end of the line but within the input buffer.

Wait, but the crash is a global-buffer-overflow. Let me think about this more carefully.

Actually, let me reconsider. Maybe the global-buffer-overflow is caused by the `md_ascii_case_eq` reading from `MD_ADMONITION_TAGS[i]` when `i` is out of bounds. But `i` is bounded by `SIZEOF_ARRAY(MD_ADMONITION_TAGS)`.

Hmm, let me look at the `MD_ADMONITION_TAGS` array definition again:
```c
static const MD_CHAR* MD_ADMONITION_TAGS[] = { _T("note"), _T("tip"), _T("important"), _T("warning"), _T("caution") };
```

This is an array of 5 pointers. `SIZEOF_ARRAY(MD_ADMONITION_TAGS)` = 5. So `i` ranges from 0 to 4. `MD_ADMONITION_TAGS[i]` is always valid.

OK, let me try a completely different approach. Let me look at the actual crash by running the fuzzer with different inputs. Let me try the second regression test case.

